### Functional Style
<hr style="width:100%;border-bottom:3px solid black;">

We've met several concepts borrowed from functional programming constructs already.  Sometimes aliases are in place to make this more clear.. for example, the `flux.and` and `flux.or` just different names for `flux.all` and `flux.any`.  A version of `flux.each` that works directly with args instead of streaming input is called `flux.for.each`, aka {{site.api_link('flux.map', mkdocs, arg='args')}}.  

#### Apply, Map, Starmap
<hr style="width:95%;border-bottom:1px dashed black;">

Functional friends like **apply, starmap, etc** are available:

```bash {.cli_example .language-bash}
$ ./compose.mk flux.map/flux.echo,hello,world
```
```markdown {.cli_output}
hello
world
```

```bash {.cli_example .language-bash}
$ ./compose.mk flux.apply/flux.echo,hello-world
```
```markdown {.cli_output}
hello-world
```

The {{site.api_link('flux.starmap', mkdocs, arg='fxn,generator')}} takes 2 arguments, then applies the function to each value generated by generator.  Demonstrating this with *only other builtins* isn't very practical.. but one of the things you can do is unpack `flux.map`.

```bash {.cli_example .language-bash}
# Itertools-ish or generators
$ ./compose.mk flux.starmap/flux.echo,flux.map/flux.echo,hello,world
```
```markdown {.cli_output}
hello
world
```

An analog to **function composition** can be achieved with {{site.api_link('flux.pipeline',mkdocs,arg='targets')}}, but the functions involved downstream (in our case {{site.api_link('stream.echo',mkdocs)}}) need to accept pipes.

```bash {.cli_example .language-bash}
# Pipelines are function composition.
#
$ ./compose.mk flux.pipeline/flux.echo/hello,stream.echo
```
```markdown {.cli_output}
hello
```

#### Fold & Reduce
<hr style="width:95%;border-bottom:1px dashed black;">

A **left-fold** over a stream is available with {{site.api_link('flux.fold', mkdocs, arg='reducer,init')}}.  It reads one line per item from stdin, threading an *accumulator* through the `<reducer>` target: the accumulator is passed on the reducer's **stdin**, the current line is passed as the `val` env-var, and the reducer prints the new accumulator.  The accumulator starts as `<init>` (or the `acc` env-var), defaulting to empty.

This is the exact shape that the [`stream.*` reducers]({{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/standard-lib) already have, so they drop in as reducers with no glue.  For example, {{site.api_link('stream.json.array.append', mkdocs)}} (which reads an array on stdin plus a `val`) folds a stream straight into a JSON array:

```bash {.cli_example .language-bash}
# fold a stream into a JSON array, reusing a stdlib reducer as-is
$ printf 'a\nb\nc\n' | ./compose.mk flux.fold/stream.json.array.append,[]
```
```json {.cli_output}
[
  "a",
  "b",
  "c"
]
```

A *scalar* reducer just reads the accumulator from stdin instead (via `${stream.stdin}`), with the current element in `val`; for instance an integer-summing reducer used as `flux.fold/add,0` over a column of numbers.

The {{site.api_link('flux.reduce', mkdocs, arg='reducer')}} variant is the same thing seeded by the **first** line instead of an explicit init (and it errors on empty input), so `reduce` is just `fold` with the head as the starting accumulator.

